    comparable_slugs: ["call-of-duty-modern-warfare-iii", "rainbow-six-siege", "xdefiant"],
    overview_html: `<p>Call of Duty: Warzone operates on a seasonal battle pass structure, with each season lasting approximately 6 weeks. The free pass provides 10 tiers of rewards, while the paid pass ($9.99) offers 100 tiers. The paid pass's cost structure is reasonable compared to competitors, but the rapid 6-week seasons mean constant monetization pressure if players want consecutive season progression. COD Points are the premium currency and are used for cosmetics, bundles, and the battle pass itself.</p><p>Cosmetic bundle pricing is aggressive ($15-25 per bundle), though battle pass content is mostly cosmetic with occasional functional weapon blueprints. The game encourages spending through FOMO mechanics: limited-time bundles disappear after seasons, incentivizing purchase before rotation. Free-to-play is viable, but seasonal cosmetics create perceived disadvantage.</p>`,
    pity_explanation_html: `<p>Warzone's battle pass has no pity system—progression is linear and guaranteed based on playtime. Unlike loot-based games, the pass rewards are fixed, eliminating randomness entirely. This makes the battle pass a predictable purchase where you know exactly what you're getting.</p>`,
    history_html: `<p><strong>2020:</strong> Call of Duty: Warzone launches free-to-play with seasonal battle pass structure and cosmetic monetization.</p><p><strong>2021:</strong> Season cadence shortens from 8 to 6 weeks, increasing monetization frequency.</p><p><strong>2022:</strong> Activision introduces Operator bundles at premium pricing ($20+), significantly increasing cosmetic costs.</p><p><strong>2023:</strong> Warzone 2.0 integration forces account linking and cosmetics consolidation, frustrating legacy players.</p><p><strong>2024:</strong> Warzone continues with aggressive cosmetic pricing ($15-25 bundles) and seasonal FOMO mechanics.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p>Warzone's primary criticism involves FOMO mechanics—limited-time cosmetics create artificial urgency. The 6-week season cycle is aggressive compared to industry standard 8-10 weeks. Additionally, cosmetic bundle pricing ($15-25) is among the highest in the industry. Some players feel cosmetics provide competitive perception (pay-to-look-cool), though actual gameplay advantage is minimal.</p>`,
    tips_html: `<p><strong>Tip 1:</strong> Buy the battle pass once during launch week. The $9.99 investment generates enough COD Points for subsequent passes if you complete free levels.</p><p><strong>Tip 2:</strong> Avoid cosmetic bundles unless they're 50%+ sale. FOMO is intentional—waiting guarantees discounts.</p><p><strong>Tip 3:</strong> Focus on free battle pass cosmetics. Paid tier cosmetics are primarily vanity with minimal gameplay relevance.</p><p><strong>Tip 4:</strong> Track seasonal rotations. Bundles return in 4-6 month cycles—your desired cosmetic will rotate back.</p><p><strong>Tip 5:</strong> Join seasonal events for free cosmetics. Warzone regularly distributes cosmetics through limited-time events without spending.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>Warzone's monetization is moderately aggressive but transparent. The battle pass is reasonably priced at $9.99, and cosmetics are optional with no gameplay advantage. However, the 6-week season cycle and FOMO-driven cosmetic drops create persistent spending pressure that's psychologically manipulative.</p><p>The math is favorable if you're disciplined: $9.99 per season is sustainable if you complete the pass monthly. However, Activision's cosmetic pricing ($15-25 bundles) exploits FOMO psychology. Most players spend multiple times per season on "limited-time" cosmetics that mysteriously rotate back in 6 months.</p><p>Recommendation: Free-to-play is viable. If paying, limit to one $9.99 pass per season and ignore cosmetic bundles. Warzone is more ethical than pay-to-win games but less fair than cosmetic-only games with longer season cycles. It's a middle ground—reasonable monetization executed with FOMO manipulation.</p>`
