    editorial_html: `<p>Modern Warfare III's monetization is split into reasonable (battle pass) and aggressive (cosmetics). The $9.99 season pass is fair, but cosmetic operators at $20-25 are among the industry's priciest. Combined with the $69.99 base game purchase, total year-one cost approaches $150-200 if players engage cosmetics.</p><p>The business model is sustainable but not consumer-friendly. Cosmetic inflation is real—pricing has escalated from $15 (MW2) to $20-25 (MWIII). This is classic price creep: gradually increasing costs until consumers accept them as normal. Compared to Warzone's cosmetics or Helldivers 2's cosmetic philosophy, Modern Warfare III prioritizes monetization maximization.</p><p>Verdict: The $69.99 base game is a barrier to entry that free-to-play players won't face. The battle pass is reasonable, but cosmetics are expensive. Recommended only if you're committed to Call of Duty multiplayer. Casual players should stick to free Warzone to avoid $70 upfront investment and cosmetic pressure.</p>`
