    editorial_html: `<p>Rainbow Six Siege represents the gold standard for cosmetic-only monetization. The base game purchase is a reasonable one-time cost, and cosmetics provide zero gameplay advantage. The battle pass at $9.99 is fairly priced, and the transparency regarding cosmetic content and pricing is superior to competitors.</p><p>What makes Siege exceptional: the developers explicitly commit to cosmetic-only monetization. No gameplay advantage exists for spending. Compare this to Modern Warfare III where cosmetics feel pressured through FOMO, or Warzone where cosmetics are constant background noise. Siege trusts players to buy cosmetics for aesthetic value rather than artificial scarcity.</p><p>Recommendation: If you can afford the $39.99 base game, Siege is the ethical choice for tactical shooter cosmetics. The monetization is transparent, fair, and respects player autonomy. Free cosmetic tracks exist for budget players, and spending is purely optional. Siege proves cosmetic monetization can be executed fairly without FOMO manipulation.</p>`
